<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    Ash fall — "ash that blows like snow" (owner's ruling, 2026-09-09).

    THE FALLING VISUAL IS PURE XML, NO NEW OVERLAY CLASS. Vanilla tints
    every active weather overlay with the CURRENT WEATHER'S OWN sky
    `overlay` colour: Verse/SkyManager.cs:117 takes
    `curSky.colors.overlay` for any SkyOverlay that does not declare a
    ForcedOverlayColor, and WeatherOverlay_SnowGentle declares none
    (RimSage, RimWorld/WeatherOverlay_SnowGentle.cs). So borrowing the
    gentle-snow particle sheet and setting `<overlay>` to a warm dark grey
    below gives grey ash drifting down at snow's pace and snow's density —
    the ruling's own comparison, delivered by the field that already
    exists. `curSky.colors.overlay` is read nowhere else in the engine, so
    darkening it has no side effects.

    ⚠️ WHAT THE ENGINE WILL NOT DO FOR US. `snowRate` is the field that
    makes falling weather ACCUMULATE, and it accumulates into
    map.snowGrid — white snow, drawn white, melting above freezing
    (SteadyEnvironmentEffects.cs:96 AddFallenSnowAt). On a 25 degC+ biome
    it would both look wrong and melt instantly, so snowRate stays 0 and
    the accumulation is done instead by MapComponent_PyrelandsAshfall
    (Source/FireEcologyHook.cs), which lays down this mod's own
    RM_FE_Filth_LooseAsh — the same filth the fire hook already drops, so
    a drift laid by weather and a drift laid by a fire are the same thing
    and both wash away under RM_FE_BlackRain.

    🔑 rainRate STAYS 0. Anything above 0.1 would (a) put out the fires
    this biome exists to have, via Fire.TickInterval's rain extinguish,
    and (b) collect the 15x large-fire commonality multiplier that
    RM_FE_BlackRain relies on being alone in owning. Ash is dry.

    Favorability is Neutral, not Bad, on purpose: WeatherDecider refuses
    any weather with favorability below Neutral for the first 8 days after
    settling (WeatherDecider.cs:156). Ash fall is this biome's signature
    and should be seen in the first week; Cinderfall, which is genuinely
    dangerous, accepts that gate.
  -->

  <WeatherDef>
    <defName>RM_FE_Weather_AshFall</defName>
    <label>ash fall</label>
    <description>Fine ash drifting down out of a flat grey sky, thick enough to blur the far side of the map and settle in banks against anything upright. It is the tail of a burn somewhere upwind, and it can last for days. Nothing about it is wet.</description>
    <durationRange>25000~70000</durationRange>
    <favorability>Neutral</favorability>
    <isBad>false</isBad>
    <perceivePriority>2</perceivePriority>
    <accuracyMultiplier>0.85</accuracyMultiplier>
    <moveSpeedMultiplier>0.95</moveSpeedMultiplier>
    <rainRate>0</rainRate>
    <snowRate>0</snowRate>
    <windSpeedFactor>1.2</windSpeedFactor>
    <windSpeedOffset>0.5</windSpeedOffset>
    <preventSkygaze>true</preventSkygaze>
    <ambientSounds>
      <li>Ambient_Wind_Fog</li>
    </ambientSounds>
    <overlayClasses>
      <li>WeatherOverlay_SnowGentle</li>
      <li>WeatherOverlay_Fog</li>
    </overlayClasses>
    <!-- `overlay` is the ash colour itself — see the header. `saturation`
         must never be 0 or WeatherDef.ConfigErrors complains. -->
    <skyColorsDay>
      <sky>(0.62,0.58,0.54)</sky>
      <shadow>(0.90,0.90,0.90)</shadow>
      <overlay>(0.42,0.39,0.35)</overlay>
      <saturation>0.55</saturation>
    </skyColorsDay>
    <skyColorsDusk>
      <sky>(0.66,0.54,0.46)</sky>
      <shadow>(0.90,0.90,0.90)</shadow>
      <overlay>(0.40,0.36,0.32)</overlay>
      <saturation>0.55</saturation>
    </skyColorsDusk>
    <skyColorsNightEdge>
      <sky>(0.26,0.26,0.27)</sky>
      <shadow>(0.85,0.85,0.85)</shadow>
      <overlay>(0.30,0.28,0.26)</overlay>
      <saturation>0.55</saturation>
    </skyColorsNightEdge>
    <skyColorsNightMid>
      <sky>(0.24,0.24,0.25)</sky>
      <shadow>(0.85,0.85,0.85)</shadow>
      <overlay>(0.28,0.26,0.24)</overlay>
      <saturation>0.55</saturation>
    </skyColorsNightMid>
  </WeatherDef>

</Defs>
